# Line-ending policy.
#
# Without this, a clone with core.autocrlf=true (the Git for Windows default) checks
# shell scripts out with CRLF. Their shebang then reads as "#!/bin/sh\r", and running
# them under bash fails with:
#
#     /bin/sh^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# Pinning the endings here fixes it for every clone regardless of local git config,
# rather than relying on each developer to configure autocrlf correctly.

# Shell scripts are executed by bash and must keep LF.
gradlew         text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Batch files are executed by cmd.exe and must keep CRLF.
gradlew.bat     text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf

# Generated artwork and the scripts that write it. Both export toolchains emit LF.
# Without this, a clone with core.autocrlf=true checks the icon sources out as CRLF,
# and re-running the export rewrites every variant - so the tree is dirty on a clean
# checkout and the committed artwork drifts from what the script produces, which the
# icon drift gate then fails on.
*.svg           text eol=lf

# Workflow YAML is read by GitHub, and Markdown is read by everyone. Both are text
# and both belong pinned, or a Windows clone rewrites them on checkout.
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.mjs           text eol=lf

# Files that no extension pattern reaches. LICENSE is extensionless by convention,
# and dotfiles like .gitattributes, .gitignore, and .editorconfig have no extension
# pattern that matches them across all git versions without naming them literally.
# All check out CRLF without these lines. Verify with `git check-attr text eol -- <file>`.
LICENSE         text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.editorconfig   text eol=lf

# Never apply end-of-line conversion to binaries.
*.jar           binary
*.png           binary
*.zip           binary
